PUBLIC RESPONSE: {A}

The accredited investor (AI) definition is set out in NI 45-106 (Ontario). Relevant categories include:

- an individual whose net income exceeded $200 k (or $300 k with spouse) in each of the two most recent years and who reasonably expects the same in the current year;
- an individual (alone or with spouse) who has net financial assets of at least $1 M or net assets of at least $5 M;
- any entity (corporation, partnership, etc.) that has net assets of at least $5 M on its most recent financial statements; and
- an entity all of whose security-holders are themselves AIs.

Option A supplies no financial-statement information for the limited partnership itself. The personal balance sheets of the three limited partners and the $6 M that was gifted to the general-partner corporation are irrelevant to the LP’s own net-asset test, and the “all partners are AIs” test is unavailable because the partnership interests are not held exclusively by AIs once the GP holding company is taken into account. Consequently the LP does not satisfy any AI category on the facts given.

Options B–E each meet at least one of the tests above (combined spousal income, net assets ≥ $5 M, or entity-level net assets ≥ $5 M), so each qualifies as an AI. Therefore A is the only choice that is not an accredited investor.

